ter-music (2.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop the playback core when a front end exits. New boolean config key
    preferences.stop_core_on_exit (default 1, also on the settings page as
    "Stop playback process on exit"): every remote/daemon front-end exit
    path (q, F9, Ctrl+C, SIGTERM, a lost TTY) converges on the shutdown
    routine and sends Control.Quit, so the core persists its session/resume
    state, stops playback and exits; with the option off (0) the front end
    sends Control.Detach instead and the core keeps playing in the
    background as in 2.3.0. Both messages are sent only when that front end
    really attached to the core (handshake completed, Attach token held); an
    exit from a front end that never attached sends not a single bus message
    and leaves somebody else's core running. The branch reads the front end's
    own configuration mirror, loaded at start-up and pushed to the core by
    the settings page, so the exit path no longer issues a synchronous read
    of the core's value. With several front ends, an exit from an attached
    front end stops the core unconditionally while the option is on; local
    mode is unchanged. Control.Quit is best effort and stays out of the
    required handshake methods. The configuration contract moves from version
    7 to 8:
    existing files are migrated once and receive the key at its default,
    which flips the default exit behaviour from "keep playing" to "stop the
    core" (breaking). The config.xml root version attribute is now written
    with the contract number ("8") instead of the old "2.2" literal;
    historical "2.2" files still load and are rewritten by the migration.
  * Never let two cores play at once after a front end exits. The core used to
    release its D-Bus name before it had stopped the audio, leaving a short
    window in which a front end started right after "q" saw no core, launched a
    second one and the two played the same music in parallel (and "stop" only
    silenced the instance that front end had attached to). The shutdown order
    is now: persist the session, stop and join the playback thread, destroy the
    backend stream, and only then release the bus name. The visible cost is
    that "daemon stop"/"restart" may see the instance go offline up to the
    playback-thread shutdown timeout (2s) plus the stream teardown later than
    before, which the CLI's 5s/10s wait budgets still cover.
  * Never let a remote front end play audio in its own process. With
    resume_last_playback enabled, the start-up resume path used to run before
    player_init(), so player_backend() still returned the facade's default
    LOCAL and the TUI created a real playback thread and PipeWire stream for a
    track the core was also playing, producing doubled audio (most audible
    with PipeWire, whose on-demand backend fallback made the accidental local
    stream succeed). After the front-end/backend split the resume path only
    records the selection and plays through the facade once the core is
    connected and the queue is delivered; as a second line of defence
    play_audio() refuses to run unless init_audio_device() ran in this
    process, and UI cleanup tears down any local audio engine (stop, join,
    backend stream) before the front end exits. A front end therefore only
    ever owns content and D-Bus commands, never an audio thread.
  * Report and persist the audible position, not the prebuffered write
    position. The playback thread counted every sample it had written into
    PipeWire's 1 MB ring (~2.9 s at 44.1 kHz stereo) as progress, while the
    process callback consumes that ring at real time. The UI, MPRIS and
    session resume therefore ran seconds ahead of what the user heard; after
    q the saved position included the unplayed ring and a restart appeared to
    have advanced during the pause. audio_get_position_seconds() now subtracts
    the backend's unplayed frames, and persist_playback_session_state() saves
    that corrected position. PipeWire reports the ring occupancy; Pulse/ALSA,
    whose writes are already bounded by the device, report zero.
  * Call PipeWire stream controls from the thread loop's locking context.
    pw_stream_set_active/set_control/flush were invoked without
    pw_thread_loop_lock(), so libpipewire diagnosed every pause, resume, flush
    and volume change as "called from wrong context" and the log filled with
    those lines. The four controls now take the loop lock (after dropping the
    internal state mutex, to keep the lock order deadlock-free). The audible
    volume/pause behaviour is unchanged; the diagnostics are gone.

 -- Yanxi Bamboo Forest <yxzl666xx@outlook.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 09:55:42 +0800

ter-music (2.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Split the player into a resident core plus front ends. The core owns the
    audio stack and the D-Bus service; the TUI and every CLI subcommand are
    clients that hand it content and commands. Closing the TUI (q, F9 or
    Ctrl+C) no longer interrupts playback, and a front end starts a core when
    none runs (inside a Linyaps container it asks the bus to activate instead
    of forking a detached process).
  * D-Bus API is now api_version 4, published as org.yxzl.ter_music.Info,
    Control, Lyrics, Queue and Config. The content interfaces (Playlist,
    Library, Favorites, History, DirHistory) and Remote are gone: the SQLite
    library, playlists, favorites, folder history and remote downloads are
    front-end data. The D-Bus scan job, Control.OpenPath/PlayIndex and
    Queue.Rebuild were removed, and MPRIS OpenUri answers NotSupported.
  * The play queue is a list of local paths delivered by the front end in
    chunks of at most 500 entries (one Queue.Set, then Queue.Append for the
    rest); an oversized single call is rejected as a whole with
    Error.InvalidArgs. Queue.Get is paged and part of the introspection.
    Nothing is persisted to queue.txt any more, and the resume session (last
    track plus position) is written by the core on every track change.
  * MPRIS parity: Rate is a real read/write playback rate (0.5-3.0), and
    LoopStatus and Shuffle switch whole play modes instead of storing two
    flags. The front-end registry (Control.Attach/Ping/Detach/FrontendInfo,
    2 s heartbeat, 6 s expiry) tracks live clients; an optional
    core_exit_when_no_frontend watchdog (config.xml, off by default) stops the
    core 10 s after the last front end leaves.
  * Command-line mode: play, pause, resume, toggle, stop, next, prev, seek,
    volume, speed, mode, show and daemon start|foreground|stop|restart|status|
    reload, plus the new queue list|append|insert|remove|clear|shuffle,
    config get|set|reload|reset, lyrics, track and cover subcommands. play
    accepts "~" and relative paths, hits an exact audio file, takes --index,
    --mode and --foreground, and without arguments keeps playing the current
    queue. Exit codes: 0 success, 1 usage error, 3 no running instance,
    4 D-Bus unavailable, 5 refused or operation failed.
  * Settings: new "Reload Config" and "Restore Defaults" actions, an equalizer
    preset dialog and an output-latency dialog as standalone popups, and an
    info display page (preset, fields, text cover size and charset, progress
    style, lyric lines) that drives `ter-music show` and the D-Bus output.
    The configuration contract is version 7 (config.xml keeps its 2.2 XML
    version attribute), migrated automatically from v1 JSON and from older
    contract versions.
  * Fix a startup SIGSEGV on directory trees deeper than ~7 levels caused by
    multi-megabyte stack frames in the recursive scan and playlist loading;
    the CUE sheet and the other large frames now live on the heap.
  * Resolve config, data and cache directories through XDG so Linyaps packages
    keep their data on the host. The systemd user unit and the D-Bus activation
    file for org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.ter_music are installed only by the Linyaps
    build (cmake -DINSTALL_LINYAPS_INTEGRATION=ON, which is OFF by default);
    this Debian package ships neither.

 -- Yanxi Bamboo Forest <yxzl666xx@outlook.com>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:09:47 +0800

